# Filesystem Tapestry — Vision

A hard drive is a spindle of thread. Every file was created at some moment and
touched at another, and if you follow those moments end to end you get a single
linear story: the chronology of your own machine. That thread is real and
useful, but it is only one way to walk the fabric.

This project takes the spindle and weaves it into a tapestry. We record, for
every file across the user's data drives, the two moments that matter — when it
was **created** (the filesystem birth time) and when it was last **modified** —
and then we let a person walk that catalog three different ways:

- **Chronological** — the linear thread. Oldest to newest, or newest to oldest.
  The original story of the disk, re-anchoring.
- **Similar** — "this reminds me of…". Nearest-neighbour ordering. Step from a
  file to the ones most like it.
- **Different** — "show me something unexpected…". A diversity chain that hops,
  at every step, to the least-similar file it has not yet visited. The
  schizophrenic walk, on purpose.

Chronological is measured in seconds. Similar and Different, though, cannot be
measured on the bytes themselves — you cannot embed a ten-gigabyte video. So
similarity is measured on **policy descriptions**: short texts, one per file (or
per group of files), that describe the policies surrounding the contents — what
the file is, what it is for, what the machine should do with it. These policies
are written by a person, sourced by a person, or auto-generated, and it is the
policies that get embedded and compared. The bytes stay on disk; the meaning
lives in the description.

Once you are standing on a file, you can **walk**: `next` and `previous` move
the cursor along whichever ordering is active, and `open` hands the file to the
right program for its kind — a movie to mpv, a text file to neovim, an image to
feh, a document to zathura. The viewer is chosen from a dispatch table keyed by
media type, never a ladder of if-else.

Certain directories are noise — build outputs, caches, `.git`, `node_modules`,
the ten thousand tiny files no human will ever open. Those are **excluded** from
the walk so `next`/`previous` never strands you in a cache folder. But they are
not forgotten: they are still catalogued, still counted, still referenced in the
chronological record. Excluded from browsing, present in the history. The list
of what counts as noise is not invented here — it is read at runtime from the
shared, unified `.gitignore` that the delta-version project already maintains
across every project on these drives. One source of truth for "what to ignore."

## Separation of concerns

Two halves that never bleed into each other:

1. **Generation** — walking the drives and producing the catalog of dates and
   metadata. It knows nothing about how anything is viewed.
2. **Viewing** — orderings, the cursor, next/previous, and handing files to
   programs. It reads the catalog and never re-walks the disk.

An error in one half cannot corrupt the other. Data is produced, then, entirely
apart, data is consumed.

## Prior art this grows from

- **neocities-modernization** — where "chronological / similar / different"
  navigation was born (Phase 14, "Narrative Tapestry"), along with the cosine
  similarity engine and the greedy diversity-chaining algorithm. We reuse the
  same three-way navigation grammar, pointed at files instead of poems.
- **delta-version** — the unified/shared `.gitignore` system. We consume its
  generated exclusion list rather than maintaining our own.

## The feeling of the thing

You should be able to sit down at a machine you have used for years and *wander*
it — not through folders, which are where things were filed, but through time
and through kind. Find the evening you recorded six videos in a row. Jump from
this one to the least like it on the whole drive. Re-anchor to the chronology
when you get lost. The disk stops being a filing cabinet and becomes a place you
can take a walk.
